## Analytic target enclosure, including exponential tails

Let P_(z,x)=(I+zZZ)(I+xXX)/4, z,x=+/-1. These are orthogonal rank-one
projectors. W has eigenvalue w=(-7+24i)/25 for x=+1 and1 for x=-1.
Set phi_w=pi/2+2atan(1/7); the half-angle and addition formulas give
exp(i phi_w)=w. Machin's identity pi=16atan(1/5)-4atan(1/239) follows
from tan(4atan(1/5))=120/119, tangent subtraction and the first quadrant.

For each positive q, the128-term arctangent sum ends with a negative term.
Its exact value is therefore below atan(1/q), and the omitted positive term
1/[257 q^257] bounds the remainder from above. The program forms these
Fraction endpoints before any rounding, and combines the signed Machin
formula correctly. It rounds pi outward to dyadic denominator2^512 and
requires width<=2^-508. Using the rational midpoint, it selects an integer
k nearest to T/(2pi_mid) and encloses theta=T-2pi k. It actually checks
theta subset(-4,4), rather than assuming branch selection accuracy.

For each z,x it similarly chooses integer m_(z,x) and encloses

    phi_(z,x)=1_(x=+1)phi_w-(2+z)theta-2pi m_(z,x)

strictly in(-4,4). No transcendental equality oracle or principal-branch
decision is required. Then

    Z=sum P_(z,x) exp(i phi_(z,x)/M),
    Z^M=V_T, ||Z-I||op<4/M<r/4.

The code evaluates exp(iu) by degree256 Horner nesting at the outward angle
rectangle. For every real |u|<4 the analytic tail is at most

    e^4*4^257/257! < 81*4^257/257!.

It adds the ceiling of that rational bound times2^768 to BOTH real and
imaginary component intervals. Thus the returned exponential rectangle
already encloses the analytic exponential, not only its polynomial. The
same rule applies to every root eigenvalue and the direct target.

The direct comparator independently uses zeta=exp(-i theta)=exp(-iT),
exp(-iTK)=diag(zeta^3,zeta,zeta,zeta^3), and right-multiplies W by that
diagonal. Its rectangle retains theta's pi/arctangent uncertainty, every
exponential tail and all directed product errors. No spectral/logarithm
